From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CoreRouter <noreply@corerouter>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 03:30:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] bfdd: make the software fallback of a lost data plane work

When a distributed data plane (--dplaneaddr) disconnects,
_bfd_session_unregister_dplane() hands its sessions back to software
("Fallback to software", bfd_session_enable()), keeping the state the
data plane last reported. Two things keep that fallback from holding a
session that was Up:

- bfd_vrf_start_sockets() returns early whenever bg_use_dplane is set,
  so the fallback never opens the listening sockets: bfdd receives
  nothing and the Detection Time expires;
- software never ran the session, so its timers are still the slow ones
  of a new session (cur_timers and xmt_TO at 1 s): the first packet
  leaves after 0.75-1 s, past the peer's Detection Time.

Seen with a data plane that restarts (a kill -9 of it, or an upgrade),
BFD 100 ms x 3, FRR 10.7.1: the neighbor's session went Down and bgpd
took the BGP session down with a Cease/BFD Down (RFC 9384) while bgpd,
zebra and the kernel's forwarding were untouched; the neighbor lost its
routes 416-423 ms after the data plane died.

And the way back has a third: when the data plane connects again,
_bfd_session_register_dplane() calls bfd_session_disable(), which tells
the clients the session went down (path-down), and DP_ADD_SESSION
carries no state, so the data plane starts the session Down and the
peer goes down too.

Open the listening sockets while no data plane is attached (the session
count that starts them in bfd_session_enable() and stops them in
bfd_session_disable(), when the sessions move to a data plane that
connects, already tracks the software sessions); give a fallen back Up
session the negotiated timers the data plane reported in its state
changes (RFC 5880, sections 6.8.3, 6.8.4 and 6.8.7), sending one packet
at once; and hand an Up session back without telling the clients it
went down, with a new DP_ADD_SESSION flag, SESSION_RESUME, so the data
plane can resume it Up when the peer's packets carry its discriminator
(and report Down otherwise). A data plane that ignores the flag starts
the session Down, as before.

Measured (a CoreRouter data plane killed with kill -9 and restarted 3 s
later, BFD 100 ms x 3, eBGP with graceful restart): the neighbor kept its
routes and its BGP and BFD sessions; before, it lost them 476 ms after
the kill.

Signed-off-by: CoreRouter <noreply@corerouter>
---
--- a/bfdd/bfd.c
+++ b/bfdd/bfd.c
@@ -2913,8 +2913,12 @@
  */
 int bfd_vrf_start_sockets(struct bfd_vrf_global *bvrf)
 {
-	/* Don't open sockets when using data plane. */
-	if (bglobal.bg_use_dplane)
+	/*
+	 * Don't open sockets while a data plane runs the sessions. Without one
+	 * (it disconnected, or has not connected yet) the sessions fall back to
+	 * software (_bfd_session_unregister_dplane()), which must receive.
+	 */
+	if (bglobal.bg_use_dplane && !TAILQ_EMPTY(&bglobal.bg_dplaneq))
 		return 0;
 
 	if (!bfd_vrf_is_perm(bvrf->vrf->name))
--- a/bfdd/dplane.c
+++ b/bfdd/dplane.c
@@ -100,7 +100,7 @@
 static bool bfd_dplane_client_connecting(struct bfd_dplane_ctx *bdc);
 static void bfd_dplane_ctx_free(struct bfd_dplane_ctx *bdc);
 static int _bfd_dplane_add_session(struct bfd_dplane_ctx *bdc,
-				   struct bfd_session *bs);
+				   struct bfd_session *bs, bool resume);
 
 /*
  * BFD data plane helper functions.
@@ -636,11 +636,21 @@
 	if (bs->bdc != NULL)
 		return;
 
+	/*
+	 * A session Up in software moves Up: bfd_session_disable() would tell
+	 * the clients it went down (path-down) and they would drop their
+	 * sessions for a handover. The data plane resumes it, or reports Down.
+	 */
+	bool resume = bs->ses_state == PTM_BFD_UP;
+
+	if (resume)
+		bs->ses_state = PTM_BFD_DOWN;
+
 	/* Disable software session. */
 	bfd_session_disable(bs);
 
 	/* Move session to data plane. */
-	_bfd_dplane_add_session(bdc, bs);
+	_bfd_dplane_add_session(bdc, bs, resume);
 }
 
 static struct bfd_dplane_ctx *bfd_dplane_ctx_new(int sock)
@@ -677,6 +687,28 @@
 
 	/* Fallback to software. */
 	bfd_session_enable(bs);
+
+	/*
+	 * A session the data plane had Up goes on Up: software never ran it, so
+	 * its timers are still the slow ones of a new session (RFC 5880, Section
+	 * 6.8.3), and the first packet would leave after up to a second, past
+	 * the peer's Detection Time. Use the negotiated timers the data plane
+	 * reported (the state change message) and send at once.
+	 */
+	if (bs->ses_state == PTM_BFD_UP && bs->sock != -1) {
+		bs->cur_timers.desired_min_tx = bs->timers.desired_min_tx;
+		bs->cur_timers.required_min_rx = bs->timers.required_min_rx;
+		bs->xmt_TO = MAX(bs->timers.desired_min_tx,
+				 bs->remote_timers.required_min_rx);
+		bs->detect_TO = (uint64_t)(bs->remote_detect_mult
+						   ? bs->remote_detect_mult
+						   : bs->detect_mult) *
+				MAX(bs->cur_timers.required_min_rx,
+				    bs->remote_timers.desired_min_tx);
+		bfd_recvtimer_update(bs);
+		ptm_bfd_snd(bs, 0);
+		ptm_bfd_start_xmt_timer(bs, false);
+	}
 }
 
 static void bfd_dplane_ctx_free(struct bfd_dplane_ctx *bdc)
@@ -766,8 +798,9 @@
 }
 
 static int _bfd_dplane_add_session(struct bfd_dplane_ctx *bdc,
-				   struct bfd_session *bs)
+				   struct bfd_session *bs, bool resume)
 {
+	struct bfddp_message msg = {};
 	int rv;
 
 	/* Associate session. */
@@ -779,7 +812,10 @@
 	bs->ses_state = PTM_BFD_DOWN;
 
 	/* Enqueue message to data plane client. */
-	rv = bfd_dplane_update_session(bs);
+	_bfd_dplane_session_fill(bs, &msg);
+	if (resume)
+		msg.data.session.flags |= htonl(SESSION_RESUME);
+	rv = bfd_dplane_enqueue(bdc, &msg, ntohs(msg.header.length));
 	if (rv != 0)
 		bs->bdc = NULL;
 
@@ -1104,7 +1140,7 @@
 
 	/* Select a data plane client to install session. */
 	TAILQ_FOREACH (bdc, &bglobal.bg_dplaneq, entry) {
-		if (_bfd_dplane_add_session(bdc, bs) == 0)
+		if (_bfd_dplane_add_session(bdc, bs, false) == 0)
 			return 0;
 	}
 
--- a/bfdd/bfddp_packet.h
+++ b/bfdd/bfddp_packet.h
@@ -109,6 +109,15 @@
 	SESSION_PASSIVE = (1 << 5),
 	/** Set when session is administrative down. */
 	SESSION_SHUTDOWN = (1 << 6),
+	/**
+	 * Set when a session bfdd had Up moves to this data plane (it ran in
+	 * software while no data plane was attached): the data plane may resume
+	 * it Up when the peer's packets carry its discriminator, instead of
+	 * starting it Down, which would take the peer down. bfdd did not tell
+	 * its clients the session went down; the data plane reports the state
+	 * it finds.
+	 */
+	SESSION_RESUME = (1 << 7),
 };
 
 /**
